Bonjour on Apple's own mDNS, without avahi.

Upstream couples the Bonjour protocol to avahi: configure strips it from the protocol
list whenever avahi is missing, and the Makefile compiles the avahi backend. On a Mac
both choices are wrong. The system runs mDNSResponder, whose client calls live in
libSystem and cost nothing, and mdns_dns_sd.c, the backend that speaks to it, ships
with libpurple already and even includes Apple's <dns_sd.h> when Apple's compiler is
detected. It simply is never built, because the autotools files only know avahi.

Both patches touch the GENERATED files, configure and Makefile.in, not configure.ac
and Makefile.am. The build consumes the generated files directly and never re-runs
autotools; patching the sources instead would leave the generated files older than
their sources and invite make into regenerating them with whatever automake happens
to be installed.

01-configure-keep-bonjour-on-darwin.patch
    The avahi test stays, but on Darwin its failure no longer strips bonjour from
    STATIC_PRPLS and DYNAMIC_PRPLS. Everywhere else the old behaviour holds.

02-makefile-build-dns-sd-backend.patch
    mdns_avahi.c and mdns_dns_sd.c swap places: the dns_sd backend is compiled and
    the avahi one is merely distributed. AVAHI_LIBS stays in the link line and is
    empty on a Mac. dns_sd_proxy.c stays out of the build; it exists to load
    dnssd.dll at runtime on Windows, and on a Mac the same symbols come from
    libSystem.
